From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: read the clock rate back instead of trusting the request

clk_set_rate() returning 0 means the provider accepted the call. It does not
mean the hardware runs at the requested rate. This driver assumed it did: the
rounded rate was used for the internal divider bookkeeping and for
mmc->actual_clock, so everything the driver reported was the number it had asked
for, by construction.

On the A733 that hid a factor of two. With CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT set on the SMHC
clock (fixed by the preceding patch) the framework believed it had retuned a
fixed PLL tap from 800 to 400 MHz, ccu_mp_set_rate() then programmed both
dividers to 1, and the eMMC ran at a 400 MHz card clock -- twice its HS400
rating -- while ios reported 200 MHz. Six board sessions of HS400 timing work
were evaluated against that reported number.

Read the rate back after setting it, use that for the bookkeeping, and log a
deviation. clk_get_rate() re-derives from the divider registers and the parent's
real rate, so it would have caught this on the first boot.

This is not A733-specific and needs no cfg flag: any sunxi platform whose mmc
clock cannot exactly hit the request is better off reporting what it got.
Where the request is honoured, which is the normal case, the branch is never
taken and nothing changes.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -935,12 +935,13 @@ static void sunxi_mmc_hs400_enable(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host,
 static int sunxi_mmc_clk_set_rate(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host,
 				  struct mmc_ios *ios)
 {
 	struct mmc_host *mmc = host->mmc;
 	long rate;
 	u32 rval, clock = ios->clock, div = 1, clkdiv = 1;
+	unsigned long real_rate;
 	int ret;
 
 	ret = sunxi_mmc_oclk_onoff(host, 0);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
@@ -1007,12 +1008,34 @@ static int sunxi_mmc_clk_set_rate(struct sunxi_mmc_host *host,
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(mmc_dev(mmc), "error setting clk to %ld: %d\n",
 			rate, ret);
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Read the rate back instead of assuming the request was honoured.
+	 * clk_set_rate() succeeds whenever the provider accepted the call; it
+	 * does not promise the rate. A mux+divider whose parents are fixed PLL
+	 * taps can end up programmed at a completely different rate while every
+	 * rate this driver prints stays the requested one -- that is not
+	 * hypothetical, it is how a 2026-07-25 A733 HS400 session ran the eMMC
+	 * bus at twice its rated clock while mmc->actual_clock reported 200 MHz
+	 * (silicon: SMHC2_CLK = 0x81000000 = 800 MHz module, 400 MHz card).
+	 * Whatever comes back here is what the card is actually clocked at, so
+	 * it is what the bookkeeping below has to use, and a deviation deserves
+	 * a line in the log rather than a silent divergence between driver and
+	 * hardware.
+	 */
+	real_rate = clk_get_rate(host->clk_mmc);
+	if (real_rate != rate) {
+		dev_warn(mmc_dev(mmc),
+			 "clk: asked for %ld Hz, hardware runs at %lu Hz\n",
+			 rate, real_rate);
+		rate = real_rate;
+	}
+
 	/* set internal divider */
 	rval = mmc_readl(host, REG_CLKCR);
 	rval &= ~0xff;
 	rval |= clkdiv - 1;
 	mmc_writel(host, REG_CLKCR, rval);
 
